{
  "name": "ai-patterns",
  "$schema": "../../node_modules/nx/schemas/project-schema.json",
  "sourceRoot": "packages/ai-patterns/src",
  "projectType": "library",
  "targets": {
    "build": {
      "command": "pnpm run build",
      "options": {
        "cwd": "packages/ai-patterns"
      },
      /* `dist` (the targetDefault) is right, with one deliberate omission:
         build-artifacts.mjs also rewrites the managed block in
         design-system-docs/README.md from the brand manifest it just built.
         That file is NOT declared here. It is the one output this build reads
         back as an input, and a file that is both would make this target's own
         hash oscillate — build, README changes, next hash misses, rebuild.
         It is committed in its generated state, so a clean checkout is already
         correct, and the nx.json `sharedGlobals` input excludes it for the same
         reason. What keeps it honest is the other half of that input: any edit
         under design-system-docs/ invalidates this build, so the README is
         regenerated on exactly the runs that could have changed it. */
      "outputs": ["{projectRoot}/dist"]
    },
    "lint": {
      "command": "pnpm run lint",
      "options": {
        "cwd": "packages/ai-patterns"
      }
    },
    "test": {
      "command": "pnpm run test",
      "options": {
        "cwd": "packages/ai-patterns"
      }
    }
  },
  /* brand-boundary.test.mjs and dependency-boundary.test.mjs scan EVERY
     publishable package in the workspace, reading each manifest's `files` field
     and then the paths it names — `dist` included. That is a dependency on
     every publishable package's build, and for react and tokens it is already
     satisfied by `^build`, because this package devDepends on both. Nothing
     imports @elirobinson/ai-elements, so it had no edge at all, and
     `nx run-many -t test` scheduled `ai-patterns:test` beside
     `ai-elements:build` — whose first act is `rm -rf dist`. Observed as
     `@elirobinson/ai-elements publishes "dist", which does not exist`, from a
     suite that passes when run on its own.
     Naming it here rather than adding a devDependency nothing imports: this is
     a build-ordering fact, not a code dependency, and scripts/project.json
     already settled that shape for the same failure. `implicitDependencies`
     rather than a `dependsOn` on `test`, for the reason recorded there — a
     per-project `dependsOn` REPLACES the nx.json default instead of adding to
     it, so spelling it here would silently drop the `^build`/`build` that #165
     put in nx.json. */
  "implicitDependencies": ["@elirobinson/ai-elements"]
}
